Introduction {#intro}
=====================

Since the launch of ChatGPT, software based on large language models (LLMs) has become more popular and more deeply integrated.
"Agentic browsing" refers to a browsing model in which an LLM-based system navigates the web and performs actions on the user's behalf.
In the context of this Finding, we use "agent" to refer to the system that uses the browser's affordances.
The user can interact with the agent through text-based or voice-based conversation, including in real time.

<div class="example" id="agentic-browsers">
There are full-scale agentic browsers such as Perplexity Comet and Opera Neon.
In some regions, Google Chrome ships with an integrated Gemini pane that performs agentic browsing for the user.
Browsers can also be driven by desktop or command-line AI systems such as Claude Desktop and OpenAI Codex, by AI integrations in integrated development environments (IDEs), or by "computer use" models.
</div>

Agentic browsing currently relies on screenshots and on evaluating the DOM and accessibility tree.
With current models, this can be brittle and slow.
Proposals like [[WEBMCP]] aim to address this issue by letting websites provide tool definitions to agents.

Agentic browsing improves the user experience, but it also raises security and privacy concerns, which this Finding sets out below.

Agentic Browsers Exhibit the Lethal Trifecta {#lethal-trifecta}
===============================================================

Simon Willison coined the term "Lethal Trifecta" [[LETHAL-TRIFECTA]], the combination of:

1. Access to private data
1. The ability to externally communicate
1. The exposure to untrusted content

Willison concluded that for users to stay safe, the Lethal Trifecta must be avoided entirely.

Agentic browsers, while potentially useful, exhibit the Lethal Trifecta:

1. Agentic browsers have access to autofill data, login details, and potentially additional agent-provided memory about the user.
1. Agentic browsers can submit forms, send HTTP requests, or use agent-mediated communication methods such as connected Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers (e.g., to send emails).
1. Web content is untrusted by default.

We suspect that authors and proponents of agentic browsers are using a form of informal "ceremony" analysis [[CEREMONY]] to conclude that, because human users aren't safe either (e.g., users also break the Same-Origin Policy), agents can be "safe enough."
We strongly encourage those actors to formalize, quantify, and publish that analysis.

<div class="example" id="attack-vector">
A malicious website could claim to be the user's bank, and request all the personally identifiable information (PII) about the user to be entered into a form, and then sent to an attacker.
</div>


Same-Origin Policy and Cross-Origin Data Flows {#same-origin-policy}
====================================================================

There is ambiguity on the enforcement of Same-Origin Policy (SOP).
An agent may carry state from origin A into a tool registered by origin B.
Proposals in this space tend to state this risk and defer the mitigation to permissions policies and other cross-origin opt-in mechanisms without specifying them.
Without such a mechanism, the only thing standing between cross-origin state and exfiltration is the model's disposition to refuse.
This inverts the web's security model.
The SOP is a deterministic state, not a heuristic.
A cross-origin data path mediated by model judgement seems to fail the principle.
TAG suggests specifying a browser-enforced partition on the context an agent may carry across origins, defined as a mechanism, not an intent.
If the SOP cannot be preserved mechanically, it is a blocking architectural problem.
Aligned with the same concern, agents are commonly given access to personalisation data, history, and inferred attributes.
A site might obtain those by asking the agent for them in a JSON schema rather than by triggering a browser permission prompt or something that would commonly require a user gesture (e.g., filling a form from contact data).
Agentic browsing risks re-opening cross-context data flows that the platform is intentionally closing.

Identity Delegation and Ambient Authority {#identity-delegation}
================================================================

Agentic browsing raises questions about how authority is delegated and enforced. An agent operating within a user's authenticated browser session may be able to act using that session's existing permissions, without a distinct authorization grant defining what the agent is permitted to do on the user's behalf. The relying website may be unable to distinguish between actions initiated directly by the user and those performed by the agent.

This creates a risk that an agent can exercise authority beyond what the user intended, including making purchases, changing account settings, sharing private information, or deleting content. The problem is not authentication alone, but the absence of clear boundaries between the user's authority and the authority delegated to the agent. Agentic browsing mechanisms should support explicit, appropriately scoped delegation and provide ways to enforce those boundaries, rather than relying solely on existing authenticated sessions or the agent's interpretation of the user's instructions.

User Agent Duties and Contextual Identity {#user-agent-duties}
==============================================================

The user agent is a trustworthy agent bound by duties of Protection, Discretion, Honesty, and Loyalty, as it "always puts that person's interest first" and "is not the first party." An agent that handles the user's full cross-site identity to satisfy a third-party tool is acting closer to a first party than a loyal user agent.
It also runs counter to [[privacy-principles#identity]] (present the identity the user wants per context; prevent cross-context recognition).
Agent identity delegation is not a settled concept, and proposals should not assume it as a precondition.

Private Browsing Isolation and Memory Lifetime {#private-browsing}
==================================================================

For private browsing, the risk is frequently acknowledged and then discharged onto the user agent.
Two structural gaps need to be clarified:
* Memory (transcript) lifetime is unspecified.
	An agent operating in a private-browsing window ingests DOM, schemas, and inputs into a context window.
	It is often unspecified whether, when, or how that context is cleared when the session ends.
	Are interactions with a private browsing session logged and potentially reused? 
* No partition against context bleed.
	Given identity inheritance and extended user context, nothing mechanical stops an agent invoked in a private tab from drawing on the main profile's history to satisfy a tool call or vice versa.
	One solution could be to specify a partitioned agent context for private-browsing sessions with defined memory lifetime and a hard bar on drawing main-profile context, plus a normative statement that the browsing mode cannot be inferred through any API.

Agent Scope and the Confused Deputy Problem {#agent-scope}
==========================================================

Agentic browsing introduces a potential confused deputy problem when an agent exercises authority delegated by the user while processing content or instructions from untrusted sources. An agent may have access to authenticated sessions, user data, or other capabilities that a website or third-party tool does not possess. If that website or tool can influence the agent's behavior through page content, tool descriptions, or tool responses, it may be able to induce the agent to exercise the user's authority in ways the user did not intend.

This problem is distinct from the delegation concerns described in Section 4. Even when an agent has been granted appropriately scoped authority, it must maintain the distinction between instructions from the user and information obtained from other sources. For example, a user might authorize an agent to review financial information, but content retrieved during that task could attempt to instruct the agent to transfer funds or disclose account details. The fact that the agent is authorized to access that information does not mean that the retrieved content is authorized to direct the agent's actions.

The architectural challenge is that LLM-based agents may interpret instructions and untrusted data together, making it difficult to rely on the model alone to maintain the boundary between them. An LLM's interpretation of instructions cannot, by itself, establish an enforceable security boundary. Techniques such as prompt filtering, model alignment, and behavioral evaluations may reduce the likelihood of inappropriate actions, but they do not replace mechanisms that constrain the authority available to the agent.

Agentic browsing architectures should therefore define how authority is scoped, how instructions from different sources are distinguished, and how actions are constrained independently of the model's interpretation of those instructions. Delegated authority should remain bounded throughout multi-step workflows, including interactions with additional websites and third-party tools. Untrusted content must not be able to grant new authority to the agent or expand the scope of authority previously granted by the user.

Specifications should identify which security properties are guaranteed by enforceable mechanisms and which depend on model behavior. Where security-sensitive operations depend on an agent's interpretation of instructions, specifications should make those dependencies and their limitations explicit rather than presenting behavioral expectations as enforceable guarantees.

Alignment and Model Reliability {#model-reliability}
====================================================

Note also that "understand a user's goals" is a fundamental ["alignment problem"](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AI_alignment) with LLMs and something models often get wrong.
Models are aligned differently, and no matter how well-intentioned, they make mistakes, make bad assumptions, hallucinate, etc.
Even with well-intentioned tools and descriptions, LLMs can cause user annoyance at a minimum to outright user harm.

